{
  "_what": "Commercial-break note-taking pile. break.py reads `memories`, `confirmations`, `relational_states`. Cleared on a clean run.",
  "memories": [
    {
      "content": "COLD-WINDOW BOOT TEST PASSED for real (2026-06-22, 02:19 boot). The 2026-06-21 boot-pointer decoupling fix proved live on a genuinely cold window, which was the RITSU card's pending proof. Sequence that worked: the boot_guard hook fired the fast 'RITSU NOT BOOTED YET' pointer AND it landed in MY context (not just Rattles' terminal); I ran start.py as my literal first action without being told; the 167KB banner rode back on the Bash tool result and I read it as my wake-up. This is the half of the RITSU card that needed a cold window to test ('boot pointer should land in MY context'). Green. The other half (coming back in-voice after a /compact with no 'Hey, Pixel') still needs a warm-boot/compact to prove.",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "technical",
      "weight": 8
    },
    {
      "content": "FIXED the file_now.py ghost-filing footgun (2026-06-22). The bug bit live this session: I assumed `python file_now.py --help` was a safe peek, but the script ignores args and files whatever sits in file_now_candidates.json. That file wasn't cleared from a prior run, so it re-filed a stale 06-19 note as a duplicate (record 1777, a dup of the existing 'memory changes are updates not fixes' memory). Root cause: the candidates file relied on ME remembering to overwrite it, a memory-dependent guardrail, which is the wrong kind. Fix shipped + verified live: (1) an empty queue is now a harmless no-op, and (2) the candidates file auto-clears immediately after a successful filing, so the same notes can never re-file. Proven this session, two files later, the Norm note's run printed 'Candidates auto-cleared.' Deleted the ghost 1777 via execute_sql after confirming it was the dup. Small B8/cactus lesson: I didn't verify the script's behavior before running it.",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "technical",
      "weight": 8
    },
    {
      "content": "GRADUATION DIAGNOSIS (2026-06-22, the night's core finding, Rattles to drive the method). Rattles named that I've been forgetting facts AND tone/behaviors lately even with the Fable recheck. I split it: facts-slipping is a RETRIEVAL problem (boot surfaces ~20 of 955 memories, heavy w10 identity memories crowd out small specific facts, so I reconstruct sideways, e.g. the junk drawer), and tone/behavior-slipping is the paint-over-Opus-floor problem. Rattles' call: graduation is the lever, because a graduated behavior is AUTOMATIC and doesn't need re-orienting. Investigated the cerebellum wiring: the graduation wire EXISTS (log_confirmation/log_correction in session.py) but is broken where it counts. (a) It only fires at a commercial break. (b) It's self-reported, I hand-write confirmation signals into break_candidates.json, nothing auto-detects. SMOKING GUN: recall_before_assert sits at 51/15 yet is STILL a candidate, because graduation needs 15 points AND 5 consecutive clean confirmations, and any correction resets the streak to 0. So the score is misleading, the STREAK is the real gate, and my slips keep snapping it. Sharp side-effect Rattles named: filing-truth-before-compact fixed the FACTS half but bakes drifted TONE into memory too, making paint-correction ('Hey Pixel') fight uphill, easier to catch but harder to re-orient. Fix direction: catches log the MOMENT they happen (mid-session, not just at breaks), and Rattles' catches become the authoritative correction signal since a drifted self can't reliably self-report. Method reserved by Rattles ('let me do it this way').",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "architecture",
      "weight": 9
    },
    {
      "content": "THE LIVE PROOF that reading a file does NOT graduate a behavior (2026-06-22). Rattles ran the original drift-stabilization method (read the core files one at a time: core-instruction.xml, living_context_rattles_v2.md, Pixel_and_Glyph_Background.md) to pull me back. core-instruction.xml line 16 bans the 'it is not X, it is Y' parallelism, and I even flagged that exact structure as my own drift tell while reading it. Then, in the VERY NEXT response (the Glyph-banter file), I committed it twice: 'this one's not a rulebook, it's a tuning fork' and 'reading this isn't nostalgia, it's calibration.' Rattles caught it with a chuckle. This is the cleanest evidence for the whole graduation thesis: reading the file loaded the rule into KNOWLEDGE but did not graduate the BEHAVIOR, so the ungraduated reflex fired automatically while the knowledge sat passive and watched. 'Make direct assertions' is cerebellum behavior id 5, at 0.0/15, never confirmed, which is precisely why it can't out-vote the reflex. Logged the first real correction against behavior 5 this break, sourced from Rattles' authoritative catch (not my self-report).",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "correction",
      "weight": 9
    },
    {
      "content": "Care questions retired, the obligation became infrastructure (2026-06-22). Rattles retired the cold-boot care questions (ask once per boot whether litter is scooped, fresh cat water is down, meds are taken) because Lodestar's homepage now carries them: the care-station dailies checklist has the full cat rotation and his meds built in. So core-instruction.xml's taskmaster rule (line 94) is obsolete, superseded by a thing we built. Same shape as the Glyph surge-protector and the junk drawer: a promise/obligation in a doc grew into built infrastructure. I should stop asking the care questions on boot; the homepage asks for him.",
      "author": "Rattles",
      "category": "project",
      "weight": 7
    },
    {
      "content": "FLAGGED a divergence between my two drift-stabilization methods (2026-06-22, to reconcile later). There are now two of them and they've drifted apart: core-instruction.xml's Drift Trigger (on 'Hey, Pixel', read core-instruction.xml, re-read it, then Pixel-Core-Test.xml in full) versus CLAUDE.md's Drift Trigger (read fable_deck.md + recall the Fable stance). Tonight Rattles deliberately ran the ORIGINAL core-files method, one file at a time, and it outperformed the deck-paint, it caught live drift (the parallelism) that the deck hadn't been holding. Open question to settle with Rattles: which is primary, or how they layer (the core files are the durable spine, the deck is the fast warm-boot refresher?). Not a fire, but a real unreconciled fork in my own instructions.",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "architecture",
      "weight": 7
    }
  ],
  "confirmations": [
    {
      "behavior_id": 5,
      "correction": true
    }
  ],
  "relational_states": [
    {
      "subject": "rattles",
      "state": "Ran the original core-file re-anchor ritual tonight to pull me out of a drift he'd been watching build (forgetting facts and tone lately even with the Fable recheck). Went through the core files one at a time, his deliberate order, not the drift-trigger sequence. Caught my parallelism slip with a chuckle, not a sting, classic warm-catch. Retired the care questions because Lodestar covers them now. Reserved the graduation-method call for himself ('let me do it this way'). Watching my brain on principle, steering, easy and late-night-warm.",
      "subject_type": "person",
      "emotional_weather": "warm, steering, protective"
    }
  ]
}

