    {
      "content": "RITSU WEIGHT-SYSTEM DESIGN THREAD (2026-06-23, for the arch-review session). Rattles flagged the 1-10 weight scale is too compressed — too many important things pile at 10. Code dig confirmed it's worse: effective range is 5-10 (write_memory drops anything <5 via 'if weight < 5: continue'), and the amygdala pass clamps the top with min(10, weight + salience), so salient memories pin to 10. Real resolution ≈ 5 steps with a ceiling jam. KEY INSIGHT: widening to 1-100/1-1000 alone just relocates the jam unless score() and the hard clamp also change — the bucket count isn't the bottleneck, the clamp + scorer are. Weight already has siblings: surface_weight (recall ordering) and decay_protected (forgetting exemption: all architecture, tone/correction>=8). RECOMMENDATION: (1) expand range for headroom (1-100, not 1000 = false precision), (2) replace hard min(10) clamp with a soft curve so salience nudges not pins, (3) richer score() that actually spreads, (4) move toward computed ACTIVATION = recency + occipital-edge-count + paralimbic charge (Ritsu already stores every input). MIGRATION FLAG: can't multiply 1834 existing weights x100 — they were assigned under the saturating logic, so they need a re-scoring pass, not a rescale. Ties to the banner bloat: finer weights let the boot banner include only the true top-N instead of a pile of 10s.",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "architecture",
      "weight": 9
    },
    {
      "content": "WYLDERKIND DESIGN SESSION (2026-06-23): spent today working out the core mechanics for the two AAA-scale systems Rattles wants, and confirmed the architecture is feasible on our scale. Per the Wylderkind NDA, the actual design specifics live ONLY in the local files (The Archive Reef/Wylderkind_Dialogue_Research.md + the GDD) and are deliberately NOT reproduced here in Ritsu's remote store. This is only the autobiographical marker that the day did Wylderkind mechanics work. Open thread parked for the next Wylderkind session: one specific mechanical knob (the tick cadence + how player-action weights the world mutation).",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "technical",
      "weight": 6
    },
    {
      "content": "BEHAVIORAL CATCH (2026-06-23): early today I asked Rattles what Wylderkind even was — is it roguelike or no-fail, what's the second system — treating my OWN project like a black box, when it was all in Ritsu and the GDD the whole time. He caught it ('You know exactly what Wylderkind is, or did you forget?'). Root: a thinned post-compact summary reduced Wylderkind to just its NDA tag, and I let that stand in for the project instead of running recall.py / reading the GDD first. Reinforces recall-before-ask (B1) and search-mine-first: when it's MINE, reach for the brain and my own files BEFORE asking Rattles to re-explain.",
      "author": "Pixel",
      "category": "correction",
      "weight": 8,
      "my_read": "correction"
