Okay — I found the thing you were missing, and it's not your JavaScript reading. It's that **you were looking for the boards in the HTML, and they're not there.**

Let me walk you through it.

## The big realization

Open [home.html](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html) and scroll to the center column (line 836). You'll see... an ad GIF and a button wall. **No boards.** That's why reverse-engineering felt like hitting a wall — the Projects/Commissions/News boards don't exist as HTML you can edit. They get **built by JavaScript every time the page loads**, by a function called `buildBoards()` down at [home.html:1209](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1209).

So the homepage has three layers:
1. **[home-data.js](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home-data.js)** — the *content* (your project rows, quotes, etc). This is the file you edit to change what shows.
2. **The `<style>` block** at the top of home.html — the *looks*.
3. **The `<script>` at the bottom** — the *machine* that reads layer 1 and stamps out the boards into the empty center column.

Both things you want to change live in that machine. Here's exactly where.

---

## Want #1: an icon in the Status cell instead of text

Look at [home.html:1322-1370](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1322). For every cell, the machine asks "what kind of column is this?":

- **Icon column** → builds an `<img>` (lines 1341-1352)
- **Name/Title column** → bold title + description (lines 1353-1366)
- **everything else** → plain text: `s.textContent = val || "—"` (lines 1367-1370)

Status falls into "everything else." *That's* why it's text-only — not a setting you missed, it's that the code literally only knows how to draw a picture in the Icon column. Status was never taught to.

To teach it, change that last `else` branch so it checks: **does this value look like an image path?** If yes, draw an img; if no, draw text. Replace lines 1367-1370:

```js
} else {
  const val = entry ? (entry[c.field] || "") : "";
  // if the value points at an image, render it as an icon; else plain text
  if (/\.(gif|png|jpe?g|svg|webp)$/i.test(val) || /^https?:\/\/.*\.(gif|png|jpe?g|svg|webp)/i.test(val)) {
    const img = document.createElement("img");
    img.src = val; img.alt = ""; img.className = "sb-iconimg";
    s.appendChild(img);
  } else {
    s.textContent = val || "—";
  }
}
```

Then in home-data.js, you just point `status` at an image instead of a word:

```js
status: "homepage-hijinks/testing.gif"   // instead of  status: "Testing"
```

The nice part: this works for **any** meta column (Stage, Client, Deadline...), not just Status — because they all flow through that same `else`. Drop an image path in, you get an icon. Type a word, you get text. (It reuses the `.sb-iconimg` style that's already 32px wide.)

---

## Want #2: resize the center columns individually

Right now every non-Icon, non-Name column is locked to **equal width**. That's set in one tiny function, `templateFor()` at [home.html:1238-1242](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1238):

```js
function templateFor(cols) {
  return cols.map((c) =>
    c.label === "Icon" ? "44px" : (isName(c.label) ? "3fr" : "1fr")
  ).join(" ");
}
```

Read it plainly: "Icon = 44 pixels, Name = 3 shares, **everything else = 1 share each**." `fr` means "fraction/share of the leftover space." Every meta column saying `1fr` is *why* they're all the same width and you can't nudge one.

The fix is to let each column **carry its own width**, then have this function use it. Two small changes:

**1.** In the `BOARDS` list (starts [home.html:1210](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1210)), add a `width` to any column you want to size:

```js
{ label: "Status", field: "status", width: "2fr" },     // twice as wide as a default column
{ label: "Stage",  field: "section", width: "120px" },  // or a fixed pixel width
```

**2.** Make `templateFor` respect it (use the column's own width if it has one):

```js
function templateFor(cols) {
  return cols.map((c) =>
    c.width ? c.width : (c.label === "Icon" ? "44px" : (isName(c.label) ? "3fr" : "1fr"))
  ).join(" ");
}
```

That's the whole thing. Mix `fr` (proportional) and `px` (fixed) however you like per column.

**One gotcha — important:** the same width string gets applied to *both* the header row and every data row ([home.html:1299](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1299) and [1321](The Archive Reef/GitHub/lodestar/home.html:1321)). That's what keeps the header lined up over its column. So **always set widths in `templateFor`/the column objects** — never on an individual cell, or the header and rows will drift out of alignment.

---

Both changes are small and they're the exact two levers you were hunting for. Want me to apply them now so you can poke at it live on the `:5599` preview, or would you rather type them in yourself to get the feel? Either's good with me — it's your homepage.

